      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>(1) In many large cities, empty rooftops, old warehouses and even underground car parks are being turned into farms. Known as urban farming, this movement has grown quickly over the past decade as city residents become more interested in where their food comes from. One of its most striking forms is the vertical farm, in which plants grow in stacked trays inside a building instead of in open fields.</p><p>(2) Vertical farms rely on technology rather than on sunshine and soil. Sensors measure the temperature, the humidity and the amount of nutrients in the water, and a computer adjusts the conditions automatically. LED lamps give the plants exactly the light they need. Because the water is reused in a closed system, some farms of this kind are said to use as much as 90 per cent less water than traditional fields.</p><p>(3) The benefits for city residents are easy to see. Leafy vegetables and herbs travel only a few kilometres from the farm to the shop, so they reach customers within hours of being picked and stay fresh longer. Growing indoors also means that crops are protected from storms, drought and insects, so farmers rarely need chemical pesticides. In addition, these farms create new kinds of jobs for technicians and data analysts.</p><p>(4) However, the system is far from perfect. The equipment is expensive and the lamps consume a great deal of electricity, which makes the food more costly than the vegetables on a market stall. A small lettuce from a vertical farm may cost nearly twice as much. Moreover, only a limited range of plants grow well in such conditions. Crops such as rice, wheat and potatoes, which feed most of the world, need far more space and energy than a tower of trays can offer.</p><p>(5) For this reason, most experts see urban farms as a supplement to traditional agriculture, not a replacement for it. They can bring fresh produce to crowded neighbourhoods, reduce waste and teach children where food comes from. Yet the countryside will still be needed to produce the staple foods that cities depend on. The future of food, many believe, lies in cooperation between the two rather than competition.</p>",
