      "id": "ta3-17",
      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>(1) Ten years ago, working from home was a rare privilege granted to a few employees. Today, millions of people spend at least part of the week at a kitchen table or a spare-room desk instead of in an office. Supporters praise the change as a long-overdue step towards a healthier balance between work and life. Yet the picture is more complicated than enthusiastic headlines suggest.</p><p>(2) The most obvious advantage is time. Without a daily commute, workers can reclaim an hour or more that was once spent on crowded trains or in traffic. Many use these extra minutes to exercise, cook proper meals or simply sleep a little longer. Employers benefit too. Some firms report that staff complete certain tasks with fewer interruptions at home, and companies can hire talented people who live far from the head office, so they are no longer limited to those who can reach one building by nine o'clock.</p><p>(3) However, remote work brings hidden costs. The boundary between work and rest, once marked by the physical journey home, becomes blurred. A laptop on the sofa invites one more email at ten o'clock at night, and some people find themselves working longer hours than they did in the office. Loneliness is another danger. Casual conversations by the coffee machine, which seem trivial, are often where friendships form and ideas are exchanged. Video calls, while useful, are scheduled and tiring, and they rarely recreate that spontaneous contact. Younger employees, who learn mostly by watching experienced colleagues, may suffer most.</p><p>(4) Because of these problems, many organisations are now experimenting with hybrid arrangements, in which staff come to the office two or three days a week. The aim is to keep the freedom of home working while preserving the teamwork that happens face to face. Success, however, depends less on the number of days than on how they are used. Offices full of people silently typing on laptops, exactly as they would at home, waste the very reason for meeting. Managers who judge staff by visible hours rather than by results are likely to miss the point. Companies that agree on clear rules, such as no messages after six o'clock, tend to avoid the worst of these problems.</p><p>(5) In the end, there is no single formula that suits everyone. For a parent with young children, a long commute may be a heavy burden; for a student living in a small shared room, home can be the hardest place to concentrate. The most sensible approach is therefore to treat flexibility as a tool rather than a goal, and to ask each time what kind of work needs to be done and where it is done best.</p>",
